HST Office: upstream sc tests expect Calc's formula syntax, error values, function list and fonts; they now expect HST's.

hst-sheets-excel-formula-conventions.patch makes Excel A1 the default UI formula syntax, so the expressions
that ScCondFormatHelper::GetExpression and ScConditionEntry::GetExpression print read Sheet1!$B2 and
Sheet2!$A$2 instead of $Sheet1.$B2 and $Sheet2.$A$2 (testTdf104026, testCondFormatImportCellIs,
testCondFormatExportCellIs). The references themselves are unchanged.

The same syntax prints imported formulas the Excel way: Comparison!$I:$J (testTdf131536), the intersection
operator as a space (testTdf160371), 'Новый.лист'!A1 (testNonAsciiWithDotXLSX), Calc's CEILING as CEILING.ODF
(testTdf143809), external references as ['file://host/share/file.xlsx']Sheet1!A1:B5 (testTdf118990), a one-cell
range of AutoSum as the cell, =SUM(B13,B10) (testAutoSum), =TRUE and =FALSE without parentheses (testInvalidBareBiff5), a
named range as Sheet1!$A$1 (testTdf94627), references to another sheet as Two!$A$2
(testFormulaReferenceXLS), a formula typed as ='90''s Data'!B2 (testFormulaRefSheetNameODS) and CELL("Address") as
Logical!$A$1 (information-functions.csv), an external reference as ['file:///...external.xls']Sheet1!A1
(testSupBookVirtualPathXLS) and a sheet range as 'First:Last'!B1 (testTdf163554).
Links pasted with Paste as Link print as Sheet1!$A$1 (testPasteAsLink); AutoSum of two one-cell ranges prints
=SUM(F13,F10) (testAutoSum); a copied range is parsed as Sheet1!A1:AMJ2 (copy_paste_test testTdf126421). Tests about
Calc's own reference syntax ($Sheet1.A1, relative sheets, 'file'#$Sheet1.A1: uicalc testTdf103994, testTdf150219,
testTdf153790, testTdf173608) switch the document to that syntax. As in Excel, Alt+Enter starts a new line in a cell
and Ctrl+Enter fills the selection (hst-msoffice-shortcuts.patch), so testTdf56036 and testTdf119162 type Alt+Enter.
The HST behaviour behind them is the same as in 1.0.2; only the printed syntax and keys change, values and references are still asserted.

hst-sheets-num-errors.patch gives #NUM! (ERRORTYPE 503) for a number outside a function's domain where upstream
gives Err:502: SQRT(-1), ACOSH(0) and BITAND/BITOR/BITXOR of a negative number in spreadsheet-, mathematical-
functions.csv and matrix2.csv (testFunctionsODS, testCachedFormulaResultsODS_functions,
testCachedMatrixFormulaResultsODS). The rest of those files is unchanged and still checked.

testFunctionLists lists HST's native Excel functions (PERCENTOF, GROUPBY, PIVOTBY, TRIMRANGE, ARRAYTOTEXT,
VALUETOTEXT, REGEXEXTRACT/REPLACE/TEST, DBCS, PHONETIC) and B under its Excel name BINOM.DIST.RANGE
(hst-sheets-excel-functions.patch, hst-sheets-groupby-pivotby.patch). The default font of HST Sheets is Calibri or
the metric-compatible Carlito (hst-msoffice-defaults.patch), narrower and lower than Liberation Sans: the optimal
width of an imported CSV column is 1145 instead of 1162 twips (testTdf152053) and a three-line comment is 1260
instead of 1386 high (testCommentSize). A header without a font of its own takes that default font, so an .xlsx of a
file whose default font is Liberation Sans names Calibri in it (testTdf121716_ExportEvenHeaderFooterXLSX). The empty
rows of a new workbook take the height of its 11 pt default style (hst-sheets-default-font-size.patch) and are written
to .ods as empty cells: testTdf165733_leap_day_BCE checks the one cell with a date.

testTdf107994 gives the frame of its new document a size before it freezes panes: panes freeze only at a visible
cell, and the document's grid window can be 0x0 in the headless test, so C3 was scrolled to the top left and nothing
froze (in HST Sheets itself, and with an office in svp mode, Freeze Panes at C3 and Ctrl+Home move to C3).

